   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, cloud/review-1246-opus, round 1): APPROVE** — head c8516d8c\n\nThe fix is complete. `IconWindow.contains` and `lastEndYear` are the only places the icon code reads a date. `AppIconOption.isOffered` (AppIconOption.swift:198), `AppIconSettingsModel.sections` and `select` (:68, :76), `AppIconSeasonModel.checkAtLaunch` (:52) and the `<icon>:<year>` token (:88) all go through them. The time zone is still the injected calendar's. The `Calendar = .current` defaults at the construction sites (AppIconSettingsView.swift:13, SwiftHFRApp.swift:31) now only supply that time zone, which is correct. I found no other date read in the icon path. No finding blocks the merge.\n\n1. **Detail, PR body, \"Tests\" paragraph.** It says the Buddhist and Japanese rows \"pass before the change as a guard\". That is true for `iconWindowsAreGregorianWhateverTheDeviceCalendar`, but false for `theLastEndYearIsTheGregorianOne`: before the fix those calendars give year 2569/2570 and 8/9, so the token was already wrong on them. Those rows are real regression tests. Also, the Islamic row fails on both assertions, because 10 Jun 2026 is 24/12/1447. Fix: correct the sentence.\n2. **Detail, persistence (L80), AppIconSeasonModel.swift:88.** A tester on a Buddhist or Japanese calendar who answered the Halloween question in a pre-fix build stored `halloween-…:2569`. The new `…:2026` token asks that tester once more. The cost is a single repeated question, so no migration is needed; mention it in the PR body.\n3. **Low, docs/ICONS.md:19-39.** The \"Window\" column (`01/12–26/12`) does not say the dates are Gregorian in the device's time zone, as LOT-166 line 33 does for the theme. Fix: add one clause to the \"Dated icons\" paragraph.\n\nLedger: not needed. The PR fixes no legacy behaviour and edits no GAP row.\n\n**Vérifié**\n- Read the diff, issue #1199 and the PR body.\n- Used `git grep` to find every `calendar`, `Date`, `dateComponents`, `IconWindow` and model construction site in the icon code.\n- Compiled and ran a Foundation program on Linux. It confirms what each calendar returns before the fix:\n  - 10 Dec 2026: Islamic 1/7/1448, Hebrew 30/3/5787, Persian 19/9/1405.\n  - 10 Jun 2026: Islamic 24/12/1447.\n  - Buddhist 2569 and Japanese 8 keep the Gregorian month and day.\n  - So all Islamic, Hebrew and Persian rows fail before the fix, and the `lastEndYear` rows fail for every calendar.\n- Checked the `lastEndYear` expectations against the `today >= 1226` rule.\n- Checked `FakeIconSwitcher`, `IconTestCalendar` and the real `AppIconSettingsModel.sections` used by the picker test.\n- Ran SwiftLint on both files in a throwaway worktree (now removed): clean.\n- Did not run the iOS tests (Linux).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
